## FINDING 1 (T1) — the body layer was never a crawl problem, it was a route-not-found problem

Owed twice: "fetch the document BODIES behind cb_documents.jsonl links". The assumed route was a
215-request crawl of per-bank pages. BIS publishes a **pre-compiled full-text extract of every
central banker speech since 1996** — one CSV, **20,728 dated rows** of
`url,title,description,date,text,author`, ~18k characters each, at `/speeches/speeches.zip`,
offered explicitly to researchers, terms permitting noncommercial use, no robots bar. One
request replaced a 215-page crawl and returned 30 years instead of 3 months.

**8,770 speeches attribute to an MT5 currency's own central bank:** EUR 2742, USD 2567, GBP 812,
JPY 801, CAD 613, **AUD 589, CHF 437**, NZD 209. AUD had been a dead 403 leg since 08-26 and CHF
had no feed at all — *both uncovered legs close by finding a different route, not by retrying a
blocked one.* The other 11,956 name no listed bank and are DROPPED, never defaulted to a currency.

## FINDING 2 (T1, §13) — the wired miner had been reading a robots-barred path

`central_bank_miner.FEEDS["BIS"]` = `bis.org/doclist/cbspeeches.rss`. `bis.org/robots.txt`
carries `Disallow: /doclist/`. That feed has been read every run since 2026-08-26 and card 61
celebrates it. The access boundary is a hard limit, not a preference: the row is REMOVED in this
change. Its currency was `XXX`, so no MT5 leg loses coverage — and the replacement ground is
strictly better (bodies vs headlines). BIS's own RSS path now 302s to the allowed listing page
whose only content link is the download above.

## FINDING 3 (T1) — the hypothesis this corpus existed to produce is REFUTED, on three arms

Built the tone series (`cb_tone_series.jsonl`, 7,420 dated per-(date,currency) rows) and screened
it against 30 years of FRED daily FX, lag 2 / horizon 5 trading days:
- tone LEVEL -> own currency vs USD: pooled **t = -0.777**; every |t| < 1.7; signs disagree.
- tone SURPRISE (z vs own trailing 250-obs baseline — run because the lexicon is 5:1 dove-skewed
  by base rate, so a level is confounded): pooled **t = -1.309**. EUR -1.97 and JPY +2.05 are
  OPPOSITE-signed at 7 tests: multiplicity, not two edges.
- direction-agnostic VOLATILITY arm (the desk's own prior says direction is not predictable and
  vol is): forward/prior vol ratio **0.903 on speech days vs 0.888 on an all-days control**
  (n 5,008 vs 90,165); splits t = 0.105 and -0.633. Central-bank speech days are not even
  volatile days.
No threshold was applied in either direction and nothing was promoted (L1.60). **This disproof is
the run's most valuable output**: it retires the daily-aggregated directional reading of central
bank language, an axis the desk was about to fund. What survives is the corpus (a genuine 30-year
PIT text asset) and the UNMEASURED residual: the intraday event window, which this daily lake
cannot see and which is therefore not absent (L1.28a).

## FINDING 4 (T1, live defect, repaired) — a running miner writing into an orphaned inode

Mid-dig, both FX Blue harvesters reported row 50 while their output files held 28 rows. The
shell/dead counts matched EXACTLY and only the large `has_data` records were missing, which is
not a parse bug. `/proc/<pid>/fd/4 -> ...track_records_wave2a.jsonl (deleted)`: the file had been
UNLINKED and replaced by a stale snapshot at 06:24, and both processes carried on appending to
orphaned inodes. **Nothing errored, nothing warned, and a reader would have seen a clean short
file.** Cause: `desks/mt5/data/` is git-TRACKED and this box's automation (auto_push every 10
min, the hourly cycle) checks files out from under long-running processes — the launder class the
desk has paid for repeatedly, in a new costume.
- **Recovered** every lost row by reading the deleted inodes through `/proc/<pid>/fd/` (152 rows
  vs the 57 on disk) — the fd is write-only, but the proc symlink still opens for read.
- **Repaired**: the miner now appends to a staging file OUTSIDE the repo and publishes to the
  tracked artifact in one pass at the end, so the window a checkout can eat is a single rename
  and the staging file makes any interrupted run replayable.

## FINDING 5 (T2, repaired) — a summariser that reported `n=0` over a corpus of 120

`fxblue_mechanism_summary.py` read ONE file and filtered `status == "has_data"`. The
first-generation harvest predates that vocabulary and labels the same records `live`, so the
summary printed **"n=0 accounts"** for a 120-record corpus — and printed it as a RESULT rather
than as an unreadable input. This is why the ground had produced no hypotheses: its only
consumer had been silently empty. Repaired to read every wave, de-duplicate by account, and
RE-DERIVE liveness from the data (a stored label is never trusted over the chart behind it).
Corpus now: **293 records, 111 mineable.**

## FINDING 6 (T1) — what the corpus actually says, and the two hypotheses it produced

With the consumer fixed, the retail MT5 flow clock is legible for the first time
(per-account normalised, n=107; uniform baseline 4.17%/hour):
- **15:00-17:00 UTC = 7.45 / 7.23 / 6.96%** — a ~1.8x concentration into the NY/London overlap.
- **00:00 = 5.84%** — the largest hour OUTSIDE the overlap, with its neighbours at baseline
  (22:00 3.30%, 01:00 3.25%). 00:00 server time is the **swap boundary**.
- Instrument mix: EURUSD 51.4% of accounts, GBPUSD 42.3%, USDJPY 37.8%, AUDUSD 32.4%,
  **XAUUSD 21.6%**.
Preregistered as **H-20260828-005** (overlap-hour flow absorption; dealer-inventory counterparty)
and **H-20260828-006** (swap-boundary flow imbalance, Wednesday triple-swap as a SEPARATE arm).
Both are ACTIVITY-half cards: survival selects which accounts stay visible, it does not invent
the hour at which they trade. The PERFORMANCE half (61.3% of accounts profitable at 15:00,
z~2.4 on 24 hourly tests — not significant under Holm, and the accounts are not independent) is
recorded as a pointer and is deliberately NOT the basis of either rule. H-006 direction is left
at 0 on purpose: this desk's record is 129/129 directional mechanisms failed while carry survived.

