## CLOSE-OUT — the wave-3 re-harvest (same run)

The first 700-account harvest ran on the PRE-fix path and its orphaned inodes were freed when
the processes exited: I recovered them once at row ~50 and not again, so ~500 harvested records
no longer exist anywhere. My error, and the rule it yields is in card 65b — while a writer is
known to be orphaned, re-recover on a timer or restart it on the fixed path immediately; a
single rescue is a snapshot, not a save.

Re-ran on the repaired path, which doubles as its end-to-end positive control: **700/700 rows
landed, against 57/700 before.** Corpus is now **887 records / 525 mineable** (was 111), and
because the new accounts are different accounts, it is an out-of-sample read on H-005/H-006:
- the overlap-hour concentration HOLDS (15:00-17:00 = 7.13/6.87/6.91% vs a 4.17% baseline);
- **the swap-boundary spike GROWS: 00:00 = 6.59%, up from 5.84%**, second-largest hour of the
  day, isolated between 22:00 3.54% and 01:00 2.82%. H-20260828-006 is the stronger card.
- the PERFORMANCE half decayed exactly as its bias warning predicted: 15:00 61.3% -> **53.7%**.
  Neither rule rested on it. The refusal was checked, and it was right.
Raw waves are gitignored (14MB each, re-harvestable in ~40min from a keyless route); the
committed artifact is `mechanism_digest.jsonl.gz` (4.4MB, 887 accounts), and the summariser
falls back to it so a fresh clone reproduces every number above without the raw harvest.
